text:
You can do actual html. This is Leptos, a Solidjs like rust library:<p><pre><code>    #[component]
    fn App() -&gt; impl IntoView {
        let (count, set_count) = signal(0);
        view! {
            &lt;button
                on:click=move |_| set_count.set(3) &gt;
                &quot;Click me”&lt;&#x2F;button&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;{move || count.get() * 2}&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
        }
    }</code></pre>

It&#x27;s quite astonishing how the historical inability of commonly used programming languages (from FORTRAN all the way to, say, Java&#x2F;C#) to ergonomically return <i>two</i> values from a function call has caused people to invent all kinds of insane APIs&#x2F;contracts.<p><pre><code>    [double x, errno_t errno] = strtod(s, nullptr);
    if (errno != 0) {
        &#x2F;&#x2F; ...
    }

    [x, _] = strtod(s, nullptr);  &#x2F;&#x2F; When you&#x27;re certain the error is impossible or just don&#x27;t care
</code></pre>
Instead, we&#x27;re stuck with &quot;check if the return value is -1 and if it is, check the global variable errno&quot; at best, and with &quot;the function doesn&#x27;t really reliably reports its errors so don&#x27;t feed it malformed data, idc&quot; at worst.
